/// lifetime: the heap probe that the driver lifetime tests share.
//
// A run of the driver owns its session and its analysis. It releases both on
// every path out. No other test sees a release that is missing: the
// gate sanitizers instrument the C compiler and not the x86-64 program that
// the harness runs.
//
// The heap sees one. Many runs of one command line ask for the same blocks
// in the same order. A run that gives the blocks back leaves the heap where
// it was. A run that keeps one block moves a view of the heap by the leaked
// bytes of each round. support/heap.ft gives the two views of each target.
//
// The probe watches two views, because each view is blind to what the other
// view sees. The system view does not move for a leak that the allocator
// serves out of a free chunk it already holds. The address of a fresh block
// does not show a large leak. Without `analysis_free`, the address probe read
// 0 while the system view moved by megabytes. The
// answer is the larger of the two distances.
//
// Two views are necessary and not sufficient. A round must also be large
// enough for the release that it claims to witness. A deleted `del(ck->syms)`
// leaks about 144 bytes for each analysis. A small round count can stay
// inside the 132 KB that glibc gives a process at startup. Neither view then
// moves. ROUNDS is 4000 so the
// smallest release becomes visible. A new release gets the same standard:
// delete it, watch the test go red, and increase ROUNDS until the
// test goes red.
//
// Neither view sees a leak of a block above 128 KB. glibc serves such an
// allocation by mmap, so the system view does not move. Such a large round
// also makes the block view unsuitable. The emitter module is 226998 bytes,
// and its module buffer is an mmap block. Removing that buffer's release is
// invisible to the emitter test. The closure mode emits about two kilobytes
// and is the witness of that one release. So a release is held by the mode
// whose round keeps it inside the heap.
//
// Each mode of the driver is a test file of its own, and this module is what
// those files share. One mode in each program keeps every program below the
// 60 s that the harness gives a program. One mode in each
// program also prevents an earlier mode from fragmenting its heap. The loop
// is the first action because the probe measures allocator behavior.
//
// The six modes contain 46 measured releases. Deleting 45 releases makes at
// least one test fail. `slots_free` in gen.gen_free fails only when the call
// in gen_stmt.function_begin is also absent, so that pair is one measurement.
//
// This module uses no function-pointer dispatch table. A repeated analysis
// must not grow the heap.

import capture;
import driver;
import heap;
import std.io;
import std.libc;
import std.sys;

// The rounds of a front-end mode, and the distance in bytes that fails it.
//
// A leak of about 144 bytes per round needs about nine
// hundred rounds to exceed the process's initial heap. The early
// sample comes at half the rounds. The count is therefore well above twice
// nine hundred.
u64 ROUNDS = 4000;
u64 BOUND = 4096;

// How many of the last rounds the probe holds against the early round. The
// allocator does not hand a round the same small block every time: over the
// emitter program the address runs through a cycle of eight. So two late
// samples did not find one round in step with the early round and the probe
// read 26512 with nothing leaked. Sixteen covers that cycle two times. A leak
// moves every sample, so the smallest distance costs nothing.
u64 LATE_SAMPLES = 16;

// The exit status that a refused program leaves. The mode that compiles a
// refused program asserts this status of its first round.
i32 EXIT_COMPILE_ERROR = 1;

// Measures a front-end mode over ROUNDS iterations, with both views on.
fn measure(string@ args) u64 {
    return measure_rounds(args, ROUNDS, true);
}

// The same over a round count of its own, and with the block view under a
// switch. The emitter mode compiles a much larger program. This
// mode needs fewer rounds. Its program makes each watched
// release use one round allocation instead of one block allocation.
//
// The block view is off for the emitter mode, and the reason is a
// measurement. A round that allocates a hundred kilobytes and gives it back
// leaves the heap with free chunks of every size. So the address that a
// 32-byte probe gets lands where one of those chunks starts. It moved through
// a range of 450 KB over rounds that leaked nothing, and no window or cycle
// makes it repeat. The system view is the view that answers for that mode, and it
// answers for all of it. Each of the sixteen releases exceeds
// one kilobyte per round. A missing release therefore moves
// the system view by hundreds of kilobytes. The block view remains
// enabled for four front-end modes and index mode. It
// stays steady and uniquely detects small index releases.
fn measure_rounds(string@ args, u64 rounds, bool watch_block) u64 {
    return measure_status(args, rounds, watch_block, 0);
}

// The same, for a mode whose command line ends in a diagnostic: the program
// of the checked mode is refused by the checker. So its rounds exit 1 and a
// round that exits 0 is the broken one.
fn measure_status(string@ args, u64 rounds, bool watch_block, i32 want_status) u64 {
    u64 mut early = 0;
    u64 mut early_block = 0;
    u64 mut broke = 0;
    u64 mut block = 0;
    bool mut sampled = false;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < rounds) {
        // The driver writes to both descriptors. The test captures them, so
        // that the stdout of the test is its one line.
        i32 saved_out = capture.begin(io.STDOUT, "out.txt");
        i32 saved_err = capture.begin(io.STDERR, "err.txt");
        i32 status = driver.run(args);
        capture.end(io.STDERR, saved_err);
        capture.end(io.STDOUT, saved_out);
        if (i == 0) {
            // The first round answers for the rest. A round that stops early
            // does not make the allocations of a pass that the probe claims
            // to watch. And a probe that watches nothing is green. The
            // emitter mode ran for a whole afternoon over a program with a
            // type error: `-S` stopped at the front end. And every deletion
            // of a release in gen.gen_free was unwitnessed.
            assert(status == want_status);
        }
        if (i == rounds / 2) {
            early = heap.system_view();
            early_block = heap.block_view();
        }
        if (i + LATE_SAMPLES >= rounds) {
            u64 broke_now = heap.distance(heap.system_view(), early);
            u64 mut block_now = 0;
            if (watch_block) {
                block_now = heap.distance(heap.block_view(), early_block);
            }
            if (!sampled || broke_now < broke) {
                broke = broke_now;
            }
            if (!sampled || block_now < block) {
                block = block_now;
            }
            sampled = true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    assert(early != 0 && early_block != 0 && sampled);
    // A move of either view is a leak, so the answer is the larger distance.
    if (broke > block) {
        return broke;
    }
    return block;
}

fn write_file(string path, string text) void {
    assert(io.write_file(path, cast(text, u8@)));
}

// Every closure holds `std.rt`. The driver reads it from `<std-dir>/rt.ft` and
// looks for that directory in `std` beside the program when nothing names one.
// These runs give `fort` as argv[0], so the directory is `std` in the test's
// working directory. The driver
// needs a file there that parses, and not a runtime: these tests run nothing
// that they build.
fn write_runtime() void {
    assert(libc.mkdir("std".ptr, 0o700) == 0 || sys.errno() == libc.EEXIST);
    write_file("std/rt.ft", "// The empty runtime of a driver test.\n");
}
